73,"inside_range":null,"source":"EU BREF Production of Chlor-alkali (JRC, 2014), Table 3.13 (median membrane plant: 2,600 cells + 200 other)","url":"https://bureau-industrial-transformation.jrc.ec.europa.eu/sites/default/files/2019-11/CAK_BREF_102014.pdf","note":"Model 'other' is pumps only, so it sits below the 200 kWh/t the survey reports.","kind":"energy","tolerance_pct":10,"pass":true},{"metric":"Salt consumption","model":1730.14068,"published":1700,"unit":"kg NaCl/t Cl2","error_pct":1.772981,"inside_range":null,"source":"EU BREF Production of Chlor-alkali (JRC, 2014), Table 3.3 (median of 62 plants)","url":"https://bureau-industrial-transformation.jrc.ec.europa.eu/sites/default/files/2019-11/CAK_BREF_102014.pdf","note":"Purge set at the Table 3.5 median (0.33 m3/t); theory is 1,648 kg/t.","kind":"process","tolerance_pct":5,"pass":true},{"metric":"Steam for caustic evaporation to 50%","model":0.630243,"published":[0.46,1.5],"unit":"t/t NaOH (100%)","error_pct":0.0,"inside_range":true,"source":"EU BREF Production of Chlor-alkali (JRC, 2014), Table 3.11 (membrane, min to max, median 0.70)","url":"https://bureau-industrial-transformation.jrc.ec.europa.eu/sites/default/files/2019-11/CAK_BREF_102014.pdf","note":"","kind":"energy","tolerance_pct":20,"pass":true},{"metric":"Steam for brine and equipment heating","model":0.0,"published":[0.08,1.27],"unit":"t/t Cl2","error_pct":-100.0,"inside_range":false,"source":"EU BREF Production of Chlor-alkali (JRC, 2014), Table 3.12 (all cell techniques, median 0.23)","url":"https://bureau-industrial-transformation.jrc.ec.europa.eu/sites/default/files/2019-11/CAK_BREF_102014.pdf","note":"From the cell energy balance: at 5 kA/m2 the Joule heat almost exactly covers brine heating and the water evaporated into the gases, so no steam is needed. Heat losses from the cell room, tanks and dechlorination are not modelled, and the BREF notes membrane plants at low current density need the most steam.","kind":"energy","tolerance_pct":50,"pass":false},{"metric":"Chlorine liquefaction electricity","model":72.73423,"published":[8,200],"unit":"kWh/t Cl2","error_pct":0.0,"inside_range":true,"source":"EU BREF Production of Chlor-alkali (JRC, 2014), Table 3.12 (median 53)","url":"https://bureau-industrial-transformation.jrc.ec.europa.eu/sites/default/files/2019-11/CAK_BREF_102014.pdf","note":"","kind":"energy","tolerance_pct":20,"pass":true},{"metric":"Caustic soda per tonne of chlorine","model":1.138906,"published":1.128,"unit":"t NaOH/t Cl2","error_pct":0.966839,"inside_range":null,"source":"EU BREF Production of Chlor-alkali (JRC, 2014), Table 3.13 note 4 (production ratio)","url":"https://bureau-industrial-transformation.jrc.ec.europa.eu/sites/default/files/2019-11/CAK_BREF_102014.pdf","note":"","kind":"process","tolerance_pct":2,"pass":true},{"metric":"Fixed capital, new membrane cell plant, scaled","model":97103650.772591,"published":275401597.557229,"unit":"USD","error_pct":-64.741072,"inside_range":null,"source":"EU BREF Production of Chlor-alkali (JRC, 2014), mercury cell conversion costs (PDF page 162): a completely new membrane cell plant about EUR 1,000/t annual Cl2 capacity (Euro Chlor 2010) for 100 kt/yr. Scaled by BREF Equation 2, converted at 1.3261 USD/EUR (2010) and CEPCI 550.8 to 800.0","url":"https://bureau-industrial-transformation.jrc.ec.europa.eu/sites/default/files/2019-11/CAK_BREF_102014.pdf","note":"The cell room lump uses the Table 4.4 conversion costs, which assume an existing site, building and power supply, and the rest is Turton equipment with standard factors. A greenfield plant also carries salt handling, power intake, chlorine storage and site works, so the model sits low. Honest fail, not tuned.","kind":"capex","tolerance_pct":50,"pass":false}],"assumptions":["Cell voltage 3.31 V from a straight line through the bipolar fleet averages in BREF Table 3.10 (2.86 V at 2.9 kA/m2, 3.40 V at 5.4 kA/m2, k 0.216 V per kA/m2). New zero gap cells run lower (BREF: about 3.0 V at 6 kA/m2).","Current efficiency 96% (BREF: above 95% for new membranes); the lost charge makes oxygen at the anode. Rectifier and transformer efficiency 96% (BREF: 94 to 98%).","Water carried through the membrane taken as 4.0 mol per mol Na+; this sets how much condensate goes back to the catholyte, not the overall balance. It is a typical design figure, not taken from a fetched source.","Feed brine saturated at 60 C (27.0 wt%, CRC), depleted brine 200 g/L as quoted in the BREF. Salt treated as pure NaCl; brine treatment chemicals, the HCl used to acidify the anolyte and chlorate are not modelled.","Gases leave the cells saturated with water at the pure water vapour pressure, an upper bound that slightly overstates brine heating. Cell room heat losses are ignored.","Chlorine is cooled to 15 C (above the hydrate point), dried with 98% acid spent at 65% (spent acid returned without credit), compressed to 10 bar and liquefied at -5 C. Refrigeration at half Carnot efficiency.","Caustic evaporated in 2 effects; each kg of 10 bar steam evaporates 2 kg times the latent heat ratio. Heat of dilution of NaOH is ignored.","Hydrogen is burnt in the site boiler and credited at the natural gas price; it could be sold instead.","Membrane replacement (about EUR 500/m2 and a four year life, BREF economics of membrane cells) is reported as a KPI but not added to operating cost; the BREF puts it near 1% of variable cost."],"notes":[]}